  "articleBody": "When your roof takes a hit, whether from a storm or just wear and tear, it's natural to want to figure out what's going on. But trying to assess damage yourself can lead to some big mistakes that could cost you later. Here are a few common missteps and how to sidestep them.\n\n### Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n- **Ignoring interior signs because the exterior looks fine:** You might not see missing shingles, but water stains in your attic or on your ceiling are undeniable proof of a problem. Don't assume everything's okay just because the outside appears intact. Damage can affect underlayment or decking without immediately showing on the surface.\n- **Walking on a damaged roof yourself:** This is super risky! Damaged roofs can be unstable, and you could fall or cause further damage. Professionals have the right safety gear and know how to navigate a compromised roof safely. It's especially tricky if the roof is steep or wet.\n- **Assuming obstructed areas are undamaged:** If part of your roof is covered by a tarp or a fallen tree, you can't assume it's fine. Damage that can't be seen is still damage and needs to be properly evaluated once the obstruction is removed. Always ensure a thorough, unobstructed inspection.\n- **Delaying temporary protection:** If you have an active leak, putting off temporary fixes can lead to much bigger problems like mold, wood rot, or electrical issues. Even a small tarp can make a huge difference in limiting additional damage until full repairs can happen.\n- **Not getting detailed documentation:** A quick glance and a verbal estimate aren't enough. Insist on dated photographs, a written scope of work, and clear identification of observed damage. This is especially crucial for insurance claims, where clear evidence is key. Discounted Roofing Co. provides photos and explanations to help you decide whether to file a claim.\n\nAvoiding these mistakes means getting a more accurate assessment, a safer process, and ultimately, a better outcome for your roof and your home. Always lean on experienced pros who can give you the full picture.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}